  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nToday - Friday: Windy Today with Warm Temperatures Through Friday\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery shows a negatively tilted trough just \nbeginning to move off of the Rockies and into the Central plains. \nAhead of this trough southwest aloft will help to warm up \ntemperatures into the upper 50s for most locations. This trough \ndeepens as it moves northeastward and as it does it has increased \nthe pressure gradient across the region resulting in increased \nwinds. Current observations show sustained winds of 15 to 25 mph \nwith gusts of 25 to 35 mph with some gusts getting up to 40 mph. \nThese stronger winds diminish during the evening. After this trough \nexits the Upper Midwest, shortwave ridging takes its place until the \nweekend. With continued southwest flow, temperatures warm up even \nmore into the low to mid 60s.  \n\nSaturday - Sunday: Precipitation Chances\n\nHeading into the weekend a trough dips down into the Upper Midwest \nwith an associated cold front. Moisture advection into the region \nwill result in PWATs approaching the 90 and 97.5 percentiles (based \non the NAEFS percentiles) compared to climatological normal which \ntranslates to values between 0.8 and 1.2\". Precipitation chances for \nthe weekend are between 60 and 90%. This system could bring a bit of \nrain with it as probabilities based on the latest LREF for at least \n0.5\" are between 40 and 60%. Much of the rain should move out by \nSunday evening. Temperatures for this weekend remain warm for \nSaturday as the cold front does not appear to move through the area \nuntil the later afternoon to early evening. Highs for Saturday are \nin the upper 50s to low 60s while behind the front highs dip down \ninto the upper 30s to mid 40s.\n\nEarly Next Week: Cooler and Dry\n\nTemperatures remain on the cool side behind the cold front that \npasses through on Saturday as highs struggle to get out of the 30s \nand low temperatures stay in the upper teens to low 20s. Weak \nshortwave ridging moves overhead for early next week resulting in \ndry conditions. The next chance for precipitation to occur is \nmidweek when a shortwave trough moves into the Upper Midwest.",
